100	To control the morph CSS, add `share="morph"`. This assigns the `morph` class to the view transition, which you can target with CSS pseudo-elements. For example, to soften the morph mid-flight with a [`blur`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/filter-function/blur) keyframe:
101	
102	```tsx
103	<ViewTransition name={`photo-${photo.id}`} share="morph">
104	  <Image src={photo.src} alt={photo.title} />
105	</ViewTransition>
106	```
107	
108	```css filename="app/globals.css"
109	::view-transition-group(.morph) {
110	  animation-duration: 400ms;
111	}
112	::view-transition-image-pair(.morph) {
113	  animation-name: via-blur;
114	}
115	@keyframes via-blur {
116	  30% {
117	    filter: blur(3px);
118	  }
119	}
120	```
121	
122	The blur hides pixel-level interpolation artifacts during the transition. At 400ms, the morph is slow enough to register but fast enough to feel direct.
123	
124	### Step 2: Animate loading states with Suspense reveals
125	
126	The photo detail page loads its content asynchronously. While data is in flight, a Suspense boundary shows a skeleton. When the data resolves, the skeleton is replaced by the real content.
127	
128	Without a transition, the swap is instant. The skeleton vanishes and the content pops in.
129	
130	In motion design, vertical direction encodes hierarchy. Content sliding up communicates arrival. Content sliding down communicates departure. The pair together creates a handoff: the placeholder yields to the real thing.
131	
132	Wrap the Suspense fallback in a `ViewTransition` with an exit animation, and the content in a `ViewTransition` with an enter animation:
133	
134	```tsx filename="app/photo/[id]/page.tsx"
135	import { Suspense, ViewTransition } from 'react'
136	
137	export default async function PhotoPage({ params }) {
138	  const { id } = await params
139	
140	  return (
141	    <Suspense
142	      fallback={
143	        <ViewTransition exit="slide-down">
144	          <PhotoContentSkeleton />
145	        </ViewTransition>
146	      }
147	    >
148	      <ViewTransition enter="slide-up" default="none">
149	        <PhotoContent id={id} />
150	      </ViewTransition>
151	    </Suspense>
152	  )
153	}
154	```
155	
156	The `default="none"` prop prevents this `ViewTransition` from animating during unrelated transitions, like the shared element morph from Step 1. Without it, every transition on the page would trigger every `ViewTransition`'s animation.
157	
158	The CSS animations use asymmetric timing. The exit is fast (150ms). The enter is slower (210ms) and delayed until the exit completes:
159	
160	```css filename="app/globals.css"
161	:root {
162	  --duration-exit: 150ms;
163	  --duration-enter: 210ms;
164	}
165	
166	::view-transition-old(.slide-down) {
167	  animation:
168	    var(--duration-exit) ease-out both fade reverse,
169	    var(--duration-exit) ease-out both slide-y reverse;
170	}
171	::view-transition-new(.slide-up) {
172	  animation:
173	    var(--duration-enter) ease-in var(--duration-exit) both fade,
174	    400ms ease-in both slide-y;
175	}
176	
177	@keyframes fade {
178	  from {
179	    filter: blur(3px);
180	    opacity: 0;
181	  }
182	  to {
183	    filter: blur(0);
184	    opacity: 1;
185	  }
186	}
187	@keyframes slide-y {
188	  from {
189	    transform: translateY(10px);
190	  }
191	  to {
192	    transform: translateY(0);
193	  }
194	}
